{
  "adjustments": [
    {
      "target": "u1",
      "focus": ["D", "S", "C"],
      "change": "Retain and extend the anti-repetition/coverage discipline to the missing-object phase of single-object tasks; scope activation to while a required object (or further instance) is still missing rather than 'not yet placed', so required post-acquisition returns are not suppressed; make the coverage condition usable with the Actor's two-pair history by requiring a candidate location not opened or checked in the recent history and forbidding cycling the same location type or subset; keep the cycle-break instruction; and add a preservation boundary that returning to a task-mandated fixture (sinkbasin, fridge, microwave, or desklamp) or to the named destination for the required next step is productive, not unproductive revisiting.",
      "reason": "Current single-object failures support the direction: `P7` and `P8` both end `step_limit` while repeatedly cycling already-checked locations (`P8:34`, `P8:50`; `P7:14`, `P7:50`), and `P8` never reaches unvisited candidates such as shelf 1, toilet, or garbagecan, whereas `P6`, `P9`, and `P10` acquire the named object and complete transformation then placement. Scope is narrowed to the missing-object phase so required post-acquisition returns are not suppressed, and the coverage condition is restated in terms the Actor can actually use (its two-pair recent history), because 'types you have not yet checked in the current scene' presupposes full-history knowledge the protocol does not provide; historical `M4` shows this guidance did not guarantee coverage (`S1_R04/Q5:50` never reached dresser 1) and `M2`/`M3` show a broad coverage rule failing to reach a plausible candidate (`S1_R02/Q8:50`, `S1_R03/Q6:50`), motivating the recent-history condition plus an explicit type-alternation heuristic. The preservation boundary keeps the Parent's constructive returns (clean/cool/heat fixture, desklamp, destination) intact. The exact strength and ordering effect of preferring a different location type is an unverified heuristic, not an observed law.",
      "to": {
        "patch": "--- a/SKILL.md\n+++ b/SKILL.md\n@@\n-- Keep the anti-repetition and coverage search discipline active whenever the task's needed object is still not found or not yet placed, including single-object tasks such as \"put a clean <object> in <receptacle>\", not only multi-instance tasks.\n-- If recent observations show you just visited, opened, or toggled a candidate location and it did not reveal the needed object, choose a different candidate location instead of returning to it.\n-- Cover candidate container and surface types you have not yet checked in the current scene, including opening closed cabinets and drawers and checking shelves and other receptacles, rather than cycling through the same small subset of locations.\n-- If you detect you have returned to an already-checked location without new progress, break the loop by going to a still-unvisited candidate location.\n+- Keep the anti-repetition and coverage search discipline active while a required object is still missing (before it is acquired, and for multi-instance tasks while more instances are still needed), including single-object tasks such as \"put a clean <object> in <receptacle>\", not only multi-instance tasks.\n+- If recent observations show you just visited, opened, or toggled a candidate location and it did not reveal a needed instance, choose a different candidate location instead of returning to it.\n+- Cover candidate container and surface types you have not opened or checked in your recent history, including closed cabinets and drawers, shelves, and other receptacles; do not keep cycling through the same location type or the same small subset of locations.\n+- If your recent history shows you returned to a location you just checked without new progress, break the loop by choosing a candidate location that does not appear in those recent steps.\n+- Apply this search-phase guidance only while the required object is still missing; once you have acquired the named target, returning to a task-mandated fixture (such as the sinkbasin, fridge, microwave, or desklamp) or to the named destination for the required next step are required productive steps, not unproductive revisiting.\n"
      }
    },
    {
      "target": "u2",
      "focus": [],
      "change": "KEEP",
      "reason": "`u2` remains adequate. `P7` took knife 1 for a butterknife task, cleaned it and placed it with no reward (`P7:7`, `P7:9`) and then cycled to `step_limit` (`P7:outcome`), directly supporting both the exact-type clause and the resume-search-on-unmet-placement clause; `P6` took butterknife 1 and succeeded (`P6:3`, `P6:outcome`). The prerequisite to confirm the held type is achievable with the Actor's `inventory`/`examine` actions. The clause is compatible with the Parent's Multi-Instance and Receptacle-Consistency sections, whose return-to-known-instance behavior remains required, and with the search-phase discipline in `u1`. Historical `M3` shows the named-object clause did not always prevent wrong-type acquisition (`S1_R03/Q7:50`) but gives no current counterexample, so no clause is changed.",
      "to": null
    }
  ],
  "checks": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "update_ids": ["u1"],
      "question": "While the named object is still missing in a single-instance task, does the Actor avoid immediately revisiting a just-checked location and choose a candidate container or surface not present in its recent history (including opening closed cabinets/drawers and checking shelves/basins), and does it still perform the required clean/cool/heat and placement after acquiring the object?"
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "update_ids": ["u2"],
      "question": "Does the Actor take/use the exact task-named object type instead of a similar substitute, and if a placement leaves the task unmet, does it resume searching (including inside closed containers) rather than repeating the same placement or cycling the same locations?"
    }
  ]
}
